@begin(section)

@title(A Longer Example)

@begin(section)
@title(Audience)

This example is written for build and devops engineers who are responsible
for the builds from a number of projects. These builds produce files that will
here be called "artifacts". The goal in this example will be to declare
dependencies between different builds and have the dependencies for each
project's build downloaded automatically as part of the build. Under normal
circumstances, a package manager would be used to do this; however, sometimes
this is not possible. This example in particular illustrates how dsolv can be
used to resolve dependencies between zip files, which do not carry dependency
information.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(The Dependencies)

In this example, suppose that you keep the artifacts for your builds, all zip
files, stored on an auto-indexed HTTP server called @c(reposerver), which
serves the files at the URL @c(http://example.com/repo/). These builds depend
on the presence of artifacts from other builds to complete successfully. The
dependency tree looks like this:

@begin(code)
a -> b
b -> c
b -> d
d -> e
c -> e
@end(code)

For example, in order to build the artifact for @c(a), there must first be
artifacts generated by the @c(b), @c(c), @c(d), and @c(e) builds present in
the build directory.

The complication here is that each project above has generated artifacts at
different versions. To be short in writing, we will denote the artifact
generated by the build for @c(a) at version @c(1.0.0) as @c(a@1.0.0).

In our example, there was a recent breaking change to @c(a). Where artifacts
@c(a@1.9.0) worked fine with all previous versions of artifacts for @c(b),
the newer @c(a@2.1.0) only works with @c(b@2.3.0) or greater. Since the
@c(2.0.0) line of @c(b) came out, it relies on the newer @c(c@3.5.0), and the
ancient-but-still-used @c(d), the only version of which was published as
@c(d@0.5.0). The last time @c(d) was touched, the newest version of @c(e) was
@c(1.1.0); however, the newer @c(c@3.5.0) relies on the fact that the
artifact for @c(e) must be at least at version @c(1.8.0) or newer. There are
three published artifacts at different versions for @c(e): @c(e@1.8.0),
@c(e@2.1.0), and @c(e@2.4.0). Only the @c(e@1.8.0) version of @c(e) is
backwards compatible with @c(e@1.1.0) and so it is the only version which
will satisfy all of the build-time dependencies for @c(a).

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Adding e to the dsolv Repo)

The first step is to build @c(e), since it is at the bottom of our dependency
tree. In our example, when we build @c(e), we mean that we are generating the
file @c(e-<version>.zip) using the source code for @c(e). Let's say that we
have as part of this build already created @c(e), at the version of
@c(1.8.0). We might have a file called @c(dsolv.nrdl) somewhere in our
source code repository for @c(e). We can use this file to specify options to
dsolv, including repositories, requirements, etc. of the build. The file will
be simple for @c(e), though, since @c(e) has no other dependencies. It might
look like this:

@begin[lang=nrdl](code)
; filename: dsolv.nrdl
{
    id "e"
    version "1.8.0"
}
@end(code)

During the build of @c(e), we push the build artifact @c(e-1.8.0.zip) to the
@c(reposerver) so that it can be downloaded at
@c(https://example.com/repo/e-1.8.0.zip). Then, we generate a @c(dscard) file
for @c(e). This file will represent @c(e) in a dsolv repository. It is done
like this:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
dsolv generate-card \
    --location "https://example.com/repo/e-1.8.0.zip" \
    --card-file "e-1.8.0.zip.dscard"
@end(code)

Note that it is good practice to name the output file after the name of the
file that the card will be representing in the dsolv repository.

This will create a file called @c(e-1.8.0.zip.dscard). We would then copy
this file up to the @c(reposerver):

@begin[lang=sh](code)
rsync e-1.8.0.zip.dscard user@reposerver:/var/www/repo/
@end(code)

Once the card is added to the @c(repo) on the repo server, a command is run
on the server to generate (or update) a dsolv repository index:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
ssh user@reposerver
cd /var/www/repo
dsolv generate-repo-index \
    --search-directory /var/www/repo \
    --index-file /var/www/repo/index.dsrepo
@end(code)

This command takes all of the package information from all of the dsolv card
files found under @c(/var/www/repo) and adds this information to the
repository index @c(/var/www/repo/index.dsrepo). Once this is done, the
package @c(e) is listed as available in the dsolv repository index. We can
check that listing @c(e@1.8.0) as available in the index was successful by
querying the index from any machine that can see the @c(index.dsrepo) file on
the reposerver, like this:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
dsolv query-repo \
    --repository "https://example.com/repo/index.dsrepo" \
    --query "e"
@end(code)

Supposing that multiple versions of @c(e) are in the repository, its output
will look like this:

@begin(code)
e==1.8.0 @ https://example.com/repo/e-1.8.0.zip
e==2.1.0 @ https://example.com/repo/e-2.1.0.zip
e==2.4.0 @ https://example.com/repo/e-2.4.0.zip
@end(code)

We can see that the version of @c(e) we were building, namely @c(1.8.0), is
now in the repository index. We now know that the repository index has been
properly updated.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Adding d to the dsolv Repo)

In our example, @c(d) is ancient, and not built anymore in our environment;
however, it is still used in other builds. We will not use a @c(dsolv.nrdl)
file for it, because there is nowhere to commit such a file to source. We
will simply generate a @c(dscard) file for it using command line options:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
dsolv generate-card \
    --id "d" \
    --version "0.5.0" \
    --location "https://example.com/repo/d-0.5.0.zip" \
    --requirement "e>=1.00,<2.0.0" \
    --card-file "d-0.5.0.zip.dscard"
@end(code)

Note that we can either use command-line options or config file keys to
specify the information that dsolv needs.

We then copy the newly created @c(d-0.5.0.zip.dscard) file up to the server
and use it to update the repository index in the same way as for @c(e) above.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Adding c to the dsolv Repo)

The @c(c) artifact (zip file) represents a project that is being actively
built and developed, so we will create a @c(dsolv.nrdl) file and commit it to
the source repository for @c(c). The build for @c(c) relies on the @c(e)
artifact being present, so we will resolve that dependency before we start
the build for @c(c). Then, when we build the @c(c) project, we will create
its corresponding dsolv card file as part of the build, like we did with
@c(e).

First, we commit its @c(dsolv.nrdl) file to source code. It might look like
this:

@begin[lang=nrdl](code)
; filename: dsolv.nrdl
{
    id "c"
    version "3.5.0"
    requirements ["e>=1.8.0"]
    repositories ["https://example.com/repo/index.dsrepo"]
}
@end(code)

As mentioned earlier, @c(c) needs the @c(e) artifact in order to build. We
will use dsolv as part of @c(c)'s build script to download the most recent
version fitting the requirement for @c(e) like this:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
dsolv -c dsolv.nrdl resolve-locations
@end(code)

This command is run from the same directory where @c(dsolv.nrdl) resides. It
will return output looking something like this:

@begin(code)
e==1.8.0 @ https://example.com/repo/e-1.8.0.zip
@end(code)

We can use this output in a script to download and unzip the zip file so that
it can be used as part of the build for @c(c) like this:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
#!/bin/sh

dsolv -c dsolv.nrdl resolve-locations | while read pkg
do
    spec=$(echo "${pkg}" | awk -F ' @ ' '{print $1}')
    name=$(echo "${spec}" | awk -F '==' '{print $1}')
    version=$(echo "${spec}" | awk -F '==' '{print $2}')
    url=$(echo "${pkg}" | awk -F ' @ ' '{print $2}')
    curl -o ${name}-${version}.zip -L ${url}
    unzip ${name}.zip
done
@end(code)

This stanza can be used in a build script to download all of the dependencies
for @c(c) and unzip them in the current directory.

At the end of the build for @c(c), we can create the dsolv card file for
@c(c) like this:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
dsolv generate-card \
    --location "https://example.com/repo/c-3.5.0.zip" \
    --card-file "c-3.5.0.zip.dscard"
@end(code)

Then we upload this file to our http server and use it to update the
@c(index.dsrepo) dsolv repository index file in the same way as what we did
during the build for @c(e).

Let us now suppose that we have repeated these steps for the build artifacts
of @c(b). Then all of the projects except for @c(a) which are mentioned at
the beginning of this example will have had artifacts built from their builds
and entries created in the dsolv repository index for their artifacts.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Building a)

Now suppose that we are building @c(a). In our example, the build artifact
for @c(a) need not be uploaded to the zip file repository, because @c(a)
represents our final product, and the build for @c(a) will generate an
artifact that will be handed off to Project Management or Ops for later
release. We don't need it for any other builds. While we are not (in this
trivial example) interested in uploading it to the repo, we are interested in
resolving its dependencies, downloading them, and using them to build the
final product.

Just like some of our previously described builds in this example, we will
put a file called @c(dsolv.nrdl) in the root of the git repository associated
with building @c(a). It might look like this:

@begin[lang=nrdl](code)
; filename: dsolv.nrdl
{
    id "a"
    version "2.1.0"
    requirements ["b>2.0"]
    repositories ["https://example.com/repo/index.dsrepo"]
}
@end(code)

Then, as in the script used to build the artifact for @c(c), we resolve its
dependencies and download them, just as we did when we built @c(e):

@begin[lang=sh](code)
#!/bin/sh

dsolv -c dsolv.nrdl resolve-locations | while read pkg
do
    spec=$(echo "${pkg}" | awk -F ' @ ' '{print $1}')
    name=$(echo "${spec}" | awk -F '==' '{print $1}')
    version=$(echo "${spec}" | awk -F '==' '{print $2}')
    url=$(echo "${pkg}" | awk -F ' @ ' '{print $2}')
    curl -o ${name}-${version}.zip -L ${url}
    unzip ${name}.zip
done
@end(code)

This will resolve all of the dependencies for @c(a), download them, and
unzip them. The rest of the build process for @c(a) can then continue as
normal.

@end(section)

@end(section)
